WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

00:00.580 --> 00:03.008
In this lesson, you'll be taking a look at the basics of

00:03.008 --> 00:05.918
Pydantic data models, which is to say

00:05.918 --> 00:08.080
how you can create them and how they work.

00:08.519 --> 00:12.080
You'll be doing this in the context of validating user input.

00:12.080 --> 00:14.339
So we're going to run with that same scenario

00:14.339 --> 00:17.338
we've been talking about already, the customer support system,

00:17.338 --> 00:19.219
where the first thing that happens

00:19.219 --> 00:21.280
is a user fills out a form

00:21.280 --> 00:24.280
that includes their name, their email, and the text of their request.

00:24.789 --> 00:27.690
And then the first step in your system is to validate

00:27.690 --> 00:30.471
that the user input matches your expectations.

00:30.800 --> 00:33.481
For example, that the email address is formatted correctly.

00:33.481 --> 00:35.500
So, let's take a look at how that works.

00:36.360 --> 00:38.131
All right, so to get started, you're

00:38.131 --> 00:39.901
going to do some imports from pydantic.

00:39.901 --> 00:42.125
you're importing BaseModel, which is going to be

00:42.125 --> 00:44.631
your starting point for really any pydantic data model.

00:44.631 --> 00:48.710
BaseModel has all kinds of built-in functionality for data validation

00:48.710 --> 00:50.801
and then you can build on top of it to customize

00:50.801 --> 00:53.081
your data models, and then you'll see how we're going to use

00:53.081 --> 00:55.176
ValidationError to catch errors and EmailStr

00:55.176 --> 00:57.621
is a data type in your models.

00:58.040 --> 01:01.002
And then you're importing json to do some JSON parsing later on.

01:02.601 --> 01:05.501
Next, you're going to define a Pydantic data model.

01:05.501 --> 01:08.201
In this case, a user input model.

01:08.201 --> 01:12.001
So this is a class called UserInput that inherits from BaseModel.

01:12.001 --> 01:14.802
And then you have three fields, name, email and query,

01:14.802 --> 01:17.402
where name and query are set to be strings,

01:17.701 --> 01:20.701
and email is this EmailStr type from Pydantic.

01:21.602 --> 01:25.402
And with that, you can create an instance of your UserInput model.

01:25.701 --> 01:29.253
like this, where you say user_input equals your

01:29.253 --> 01:31.352
class UserInput where name is set to Joe User,

01:31.352 --> 01:34.052
email is set to joe.user@example.com

01:34.052 --> 01:36.153
and query is I forgot my password.

01:36.453 --> 01:38.153
And then you can print that out.

01:39.052 --> 01:41.753
So, here, you have something that doesn't look very exciting,

01:41.753 --> 01:44.153
but the cool thing that's happened behind the scenes here,

01:44.153 --> 01:47.453
when you create an instance of your Pydantic data model like this

01:47.453 --> 01:49.646
is that Pydantic has validated that the

01:49.646 --> 01:52.153
data you put in matches the model expectations.

01:52.153 --> 01:54.153
In this case that the name is a string,

01:54.453 --> 01:58.352
email matches this EmailStr format, and query is also a string.

01:59.134 --> 02:01.034
you're working with valid data.

02:01.304 --> 02:04.864
Now, you can try creating another instance of your model

02:04.864 --> 02:07.353
where you have email set to not-an-email,

02:07.353 --> 02:08.943
so invalid data in this case.

02:09.304 --> 02:10.963
What happens when you run that?

02:11.593 --> 02:14.683
You get a ValidationError. So if you scroll all the way

02:14.683 --> 02:16.667
down to the bottom of this mess,

02:16.667 --> 02:18.933
you see ValidationError, one validation error for UserInput,

02:18.933 --> 02:21.804
email, value is not a valid email address.

02:21.804 --> 02:23.993
An email address must have an at sign.

02:24.473 --> 02:26.367
Okay, so apparently Pydantic is looking for

02:26.367 --> 02:28.533
an at sign here in this email string.

02:28.754 --> 02:30.199
format. So you can try putting in

02:30.199 --> 02:31.645
an at sign and see what happens.

02:31.645 --> 02:33.294
Let's run that.

02:33.294 --> 02:35.594
You get another validation error. Okay.

02:35.594 --> 02:39.685
value for email is still not a valid email address.

02:39.685 --> 02:41.689
The part after the @-sign is not

02:41.689 --> 02:43.694
valid, it should have a period. Okay.

02:43.694 --> 02:45.375
So, here's another clue as to what's

02:45.375 --> 02:47.055
going on inside the email string format.

02:47.055 --> 02:49.764
So let's add a period somewhere here after the at sign.

02:49.764 --> 02:52.865
and run that. Okay, yet another validation error.

02:52.865 --> 02:54.512
And now it says an email

02:54.512 --> 02:56.435
address cannot end with a period. Okay.

02:56.435 --> 02:58.375
But we're making progress here.

02:58.686 --> 03:02.286
Let's try putting something after the period.

03:02.816 --> 03:05.445
and running that. Okay, that worked.

03:06.006 --> 03:08.485
So this little experiment gives you a sense of

03:08.485 --> 03:11.015
in this case, what pydantic is doing behind the scenes

03:11.015 --> 03:13.131
to check that your email field matches

03:13.131 --> 03:15.246
the expectations of that email string format.

03:15.665 --> 03:16.972
So now you can see that it's

03:16.972 --> 03:18.466
looking for an at sign somewhere in there.

03:18.466 --> 03:20.300
It's looking for a period somewhere after the at

03:20.300 --> 03:22.135
sign and then something that comes after the period.

03:22.325 --> 03:24.149
So this is not saying that now you have

03:24.149 --> 03:26.175
a valid email address that's actually going to go somewhere.

03:26.175 --> 03:28.258
It's just saying, okay, this matches the

03:28.258 --> 03:30.337
basic expectations for an email string format.

03:30.837 --> 03:32.578
And so email string is a built-in

03:32.578 --> 03:34.568
data type in pydantic, but you can define

03:34.568 --> 03:37.467
your own string patterns or other specific data types

03:37.467 --> 03:39.777
for whatever specific field you want in your model.

03:40.318 --> 03:43.043
And so rather than running head first into validation

03:43.043 --> 03:45.768
errors like we were just doing with email string,

03:45.768 --> 03:48.397
the next thing you're going to do is create a function.

03:48.788 --> 03:51.078
Here you have a function called validate_user_input

03:51.078 --> 03:53.207
where you're going to pass in input_data. In this

03:53.207 --> 03:55.337
case, this is going to be a Python dictionary.

03:55.337 --> 03:58.008
And then you're going to try parsing that input

03:58.008 --> 04:01.538
data into your UserInput model. And so this is just unpacking

04:01.538 --> 04:03.929
that Python dictionary into your UserInput model.

04:03.929 --> 04:06.456
If that works, you'll say valid user input created and

04:06.456 --> 04:09.239
dump out the JSON representation of the contents of your model.

04:09.239 --> 04:11.639
And if there's a problem, you'll say Validation error

04:11.639 --> 04:14.038
occurred and print out the contents of the error.

04:14.038 --> 04:16.637
And so this is more like what you might be doing

04:16.637 --> 04:19.235
inside of a software application where you're trying to create an

04:19.235 --> 04:21.833
instance of your model and if there's an issue with that,

04:21.833 --> 04:24.668
you want to capture that error and decide what to do next.

04:24.668 --> 04:27.759
So let's define that. And then you can

04:27.759 --> 04:31.199
try using that function to create an instance of your data model.

04:31.199 --> 04:33.980
So here you have input data defined as a Python dictionary.

04:33.980 --> 04:36.800
This is just the same input that we started with up top.

04:36.800 --> 04:38.865
And then you're using the validate user input

04:38.865 --> 04:41.189
model to print out the contents of the model,

04:41.189 --> 04:44.240
to validate the input data that you've got here.

04:45.139 --> 04:47.930
when you run that, it says valid user input created and

04:47.930 --> 04:50.139
prints out the contents of the model.

04:50.550 --> 04:52.812
Great. So then you can start experimenting

04:52.812 --> 04:54.939
with some other versions of user input.

04:55.290 --> 04:57.511
In this case, you have input data that contains name,

04:57.511 --> 04:59.221
and email, but not a query field.

04:59.221 --> 05:01.000
So what happens when you run that?

05:01.920 --> 05:03.581
Well, you get a validation error that's occurred.

05:03.581 --> 05:05.360
query field is required.

05:05.360 --> 05:07.870
So it turns out that when you define a pydantic

05:07.870 --> 05:10.380
data model as you did above with these three fields,

05:10.380 --> 05:13.440
each of those fields are going to be required in your model.

05:13.440 --> 05:16.740
And it turns out you can include optional fields as well.

05:16.740 --> 05:19.401
So that's what we're going to do next here.

05:20.261 --> 05:22.100
So here you're going to do a few more imports.

05:22.100 --> 05:24.391
from pydantic you're importing Field.

05:24.391 --> 05:27.262
from typing you're importing Optional and then from datetime you're importing

05:27.262 --> 05:29.002
date and these are what you're going to

05:29.002 --> 05:30.742
use to customize your model a bit further.

05:30.742 --> 05:34.201
Now you're going to define a new version of UserInput

05:34.201 --> 05:36.432
that has the same three fields at the top here.

05:36.432 --> 05:38.492
But now a new field called order_id

05:38.492 --> 05:40.180
that's defined to be an optional

05:40.180 --> 05:42.151
field. It's going to be an integer.

05:42.151 --> 05:45.232
And then with this Field object, you can add some

05:45.232 --> 05:48.622
more customization and definition to in this case, the order_id field.

05:48.622 --> 05:51.161
So you're saying the default value is going to be None.

05:51.161 --> 05:53.108
This is a description of what this field is

05:53.108 --> 05:55.271
all about. It's going to be a 5-digit order number.

05:55.271 --> 05:57.072
cannot start with 0, and then

05:57.322 --> 05:59.648
these are a couple of rules that you're setting in

05:59.648 --> 06:01.973
order to put constraints around what the input can be.

06:01.973 --> 06:04.833
So it's saying this has to be an integer greater than 10,000,

06:04.833 --> 06:08.242
less than or equal to 99,999.

06:08.242 --> 06:09.964
So that enforces this rule of it being

06:09.964 --> 06:11.903
a five-digit order number that cannot start with zero.

06:11.903 --> 06:14.083
And then you have purchase_date, which is another optional field,

06:14.083 --> 06:15.865
and it's going to be this date

06:15.865 --> 06:19.012
object from datetime with a default of None. So let's define that.

06:20.483 --> 06:22.762
And then you can try out this new UserInput model

06:23.242 --> 06:24.822
with some new input.

06:25.103 --> 06:26.483
So here you have the exact same

06:26.483 --> 06:27.863
input. that you started with up top

06:27.863 --> 06:30.663
and you're running it through validate user input data again with that.

06:30.663 --> 06:33.163
Now with your new model of user input.

06:33.663 --> 06:35.663
And you get no problem at all.

06:35.663 --> 06:38.764
The optional fields were omitted in the input data.

06:38.764 --> 06:40.764
And so you have valid user input

06:40.764 --> 06:42.764
created with name, email and query populated

06:42.764 --> 06:45.663
and order ID and purchase date as null.

06:45.663 --> 06:47.499
So, one thing that might look confusing here

06:47.499 --> 06:49.564
is that these are printed out as null here

06:49.564 --> 06:51.863
when up here you've defined the default to be None.

06:51.863 --> 06:53.963
It turns out that this is just the

06:53.963 --> 06:56.516
JSON representation of the contents of your model. So,

06:56.516 --> 06:59.995
if you go back up here and have a look at validate_user_input.

07:00.305 --> 07:02.670
So you see that what you're doing

07:02.670 --> 07:05.036
here is using this built-in model_dump_json method

07:05.456 --> 07:07.377
of your pydantic data model, and that

07:07.377 --> 07:09.576
prints out the JSON representation of your model.

07:10.136 --> 07:12.355
If you go back down here and print

07:12.355 --> 07:14.576
out the actual contents of the model itself.

07:14.586 --> 07:18.505
So user_input, printing that out, you see that in fact

07:19.295 --> 07:21.636
order_id is None and purchase_date is None.

07:21.935 --> 07:23.755
So this is the Python representation

07:23.755 --> 07:26.315
and this is the JSON representation of the same data.

07:26.403 --> 07:29.132
your model. And so now you can play around

07:29.132 --> 07:31.153
with this new user input model a bit.

07:31.153 --> 07:34.434
Let's try user input data that includes all five fields.

07:34.434 --> 07:37.504
And in this case, valid instances of order ID and purchase date.

07:37.504 --> 07:39.944
And run that. Okay, great.

07:39.944 --> 07:43.343
So now you have valid user input that contains all five fields.

07:43.343 --> 07:45.696
And it's also interesting to take a look

07:45.696 --> 07:48.343
at what happens when you have some additional fields.

07:48.343 --> 07:51.252
So now you have input data that contains all five

07:51.252 --> 07:54.454
fields you expect and a couple of fields you don't expect.

07:54.454 --> 07:56.017
system message and

07:56.017 --> 07:58.468
iteration. What happens when you run that?

07:59.028 --> 08:01.108
Well, no problem. Valid user input created,

08:01.108 --> 08:04.017
and Pydantic just ignored these extra fields.

08:04.017 --> 08:06.997
So, this is a feature of Pydantic and it's actually

08:06.997 --> 08:09.137
a really common way in which people use Pydantic.

08:09.137 --> 08:11.571
You might have data in your system that's coming to

08:11.571 --> 08:14.247
you in the form of a Python dictionary or JSON data

08:14.247 --> 08:16.153
that has a zillion different fields, some of which

08:16.153 --> 08:18.057
you care about and some of which you don't,

08:18.057 --> 08:19.928
and you can define a Pydantic data

08:19.928 --> 08:22.067
model that grabs the fields you care about

08:22.067 --> 08:23.314
and validates those and just ignores

08:23.314 --> 08:24.767
the fields that you don't care about.

08:24.767 --> 08:27.540
So in this case, you have valid user input where

08:27.540 --> 08:29.608
you just ignored system message and iteration.

08:29.869 --> 08:31.166
One thing that you might have noticed

08:31.166 --> 08:32.649
here is that purchase_date is getting printed out

08:32.649 --> 08:34.808
as a string representation of the date.

08:35.009 --> 08:37.869
Here again, purchase_date is the string representation of the date

08:37.869 --> 08:39.816
and this looks different from the

08:39.816 --> 08:43.658
date time object that you're putting in. So, what's going on there?

08:43.769 --> 08:46.499
Well, it turns out this is just another example

08:46.499 --> 08:49.068
of the JSON representation versus the Python representation.

08:49.278 --> 08:51.033
So, if in this case, you go

08:51.033 --> 08:54.708
ahead and print out what user_input model contains,

08:55.690 --> 08:57.500
then you have purchase date is a

08:57.500 --> 08:59.309
datetime object just like you put in.

08:59.710 --> 09:01.473
And so then it can be

09:01.473 --> 09:03.529
interesting to look at what happens when

09:03.529 --> 09:06.306
you put in input where the purchase date

09:06.306 --> 09:09.429
is that string representation instead. Turns out, no problem.

09:09.429 --> 09:13.490
You can put in a date that looks like this or a

09:13.490 --> 09:15.520
datetime object and what's happening behind

09:15.520 --> 09:17.889
the scenes here is that Pydantic is

09:18.470 --> 09:21.649
converting that string into a datetime object.

09:21.649 --> 09:25.591
And this is an example of what's called data type coercion.

09:25.591 --> 09:29.421
So, pydantic in this case is handling multiple different formats for date,

09:29.421 --> 09:31.630
and this is a feature of pydantic that allows you

09:31.630 --> 09:33.385
to be a little bit more flexible

09:33.385 --> 09:35.140
with what the inputs can look like.

09:35.140 --> 09:37.019
So pydantic has a number of

09:37.019 --> 09:39.211
different data types where there's automatic coercion

09:39.211 --> 09:41.931
of, in this case, dates in different formats,

09:41.931 --> 09:44.100
and you can try this for

09:44.100 --> 09:46.630
other data types as well. For example,

09:47.331 --> 09:51.100
if you put in your order_id as a string

09:51.100 --> 09:55.203
and run that, you see also, no problem at all. pydantic

09:55.203 --> 09:56.903
can handle a string representation of an

09:56.903 --> 09:58.604
integer and automatically convert that for you.

09:59.163 --> 10:01.649
So data type coercion is something that happens

10:01.649 --> 10:04.134
automatically for certain data types and certain formats,

10:04.453 --> 10:06.080
and it's something that you can

10:06.080 --> 10:07.979
customize yourself if you want to have

10:07.979 --> 10:09.605
a specific set of formats be

10:09.605 --> 10:11.504
acceptable for a field in your model.

10:11.653 --> 10:13.392
With pydantic you also have the option

10:13.392 --> 10:15.379
of turning off data type coercion if you

10:15.379 --> 10:17.117
want to be very strict about the

10:17.117 --> 10:19.104
format that you're accepting in a particular field.

10:19.734 --> 10:22.254
So in this case, you've seen that you can put

10:22.254 --> 10:24.774
in an integer as a string, but what about putting

10:24.774 --> 10:26.543
in a string as an integer.

10:26.543 --> 10:29.073
So in the name field here, this is defined to

10:29.073 --> 10:31.604
be a string field and if you try with 99999,

10:31.604 --> 10:33.976
then you'll find that you get a validation error

10:33.976 --> 10:36.614
and that's name, the input should be a valid string.

10:36.614 --> 10:40.453
So in this case integers can be coerced from strings,

10:40.453 --> 10:43.403
but strings will not be coerced from integers.

10:43.403 --> 10:47.004
And next, you can take a step in the direction of

10:47.004 --> 10:50.933
where we're going with validating LLM output and start with JSON data.

10:50.933 --> 10:54.524
So in this case you're defining a JSON string that contains

10:54.524 --> 10:57.414
means the fields for your model and

10:57.414 --> 10:58.908
then you can parse that into a

10:58.908 --> 11:00.615
Python dictionary and print out what you get.

11:01.674 --> 11:05.544
So in this case, you've parsed the JSON into a Python dictionary,

11:05.544 --> 11:07.407
and then you can go ahead and

11:07.407 --> 11:09.534
run your validate_user_input function on that Python dictionary

11:09.875 --> 11:12.375
and see that you have valid user input.

11:12.375 --> 11:14.990
So when we move on to looking at LLM responses,

11:14.990 --> 11:17.605
you'll be getting data as a string from the LLM

11:17.605 --> 11:21.024
and you'll be using that to populate your pydantic data model.

11:21.554 --> 11:24.275
And so you can play around with different JSON input data.

11:24.275 --> 11:26.273
up. For example, in this case, data

11:26.273 --> 11:28.556
that contains order ID that starts with zero.

11:28.556 --> 11:30.385
So if you recall the rules for order

11:30.385 --> 11:32.216
ID that it must not start with zero.

11:32.216 --> 11:34.505
You can go ahead and parse that into a Python dictionary

11:34.505 --> 11:36.456
and see that there's no problem with that.

11:36.456 --> 11:38.811
Of course, this is valid JSON data and so

11:38.811 --> 11:41.166
there's no problem loading it into a Python dictionary,

11:41.166 --> 11:43.360
but then when you try to populate

11:43.360 --> 11:45.556
an instance of your user input model,

11:45.556 --> 11:47.711
you run into a validation error that order_id

11:47.711 --> 11:49.865
should be greater than or equal to 10,000.

11:50.135 --> 11:52.081
So it's sort of a two-step process when

11:52.081 --> 11:53.793
you're starting with a string representation of

11:53.793 --> 11:55.506
JSON. data, first you parse that JSON,

11:55.506 --> 11:58.567
and then you populate an instance of your model.

11:59.086 --> 12:01.866
But the way you're going to do that in practice

12:02.267 --> 12:06.326
is to use this model_validate_json built-in method from pydantic.

12:06.326 --> 12:09.136
So here you're taking your UserInput model

12:09.376 --> 12:12.917
and applying the model_validate_json method directly on that json_data.

12:12.917 --> 12:14.809
And of course, when you run

12:14.809 --> 12:17.017
that, you get a validation error because

12:17.397 --> 12:19.866
you have a problem with the order_id field.

12:20.407 --> 12:21.937
But what's happening behind the scenes here

12:21.937 --> 12:24.538
is you're doing this two-step process of first parsing the JSON

12:24.538 --> 12:27.967
and then populating your data model with the contents of the JSON.

12:28.317 --> 12:30.378
So you can go up and fix the issue.

12:30.717 --> 12:32.878
In this case, I could just turn this to a one,

12:33.927 --> 12:35.707
redefine that JSON string

12:35.707 --> 12:38.172
and run this last cell again to

12:38.172 --> 12:40.988
validate the data that's in that JSON string.

12:41.638 --> 12:44.128
You can also play around with what happens in

12:44.128 --> 12:46.618
this case if the JSON itself is not valid.

12:46.618 --> 12:48.768
So for example, if you're missing one of these brackets,

12:49.278 --> 12:50.827
and I'm just going to comment

12:50.827 --> 12:52.699
out these other lines for the moment. and run

12:52.699 --> 12:54.778
that to define new JSON data that has a problem.

12:54.778 --> 12:56.778
Then, when you run

12:58.198 --> 13:00.399
model_validate_json down here,

13:00.859 --> 13:03.009
you get a different type of validation error.

13:03.009 --> 13:06.328
In this case, a JSON end of file problem,

13:06.328 --> 13:07.929
and it tries to describe what

13:07.929 --> 13:09.798
happened with the problem loading the JSON.

13:09.798 --> 13:12.188
So, you'll get different types of validation errors

13:12.188 --> 13:15.149
depending on whether you have issues with the JSON input

13:15.149 --> 13:18.109
or issues with the data that's contained in the JSON.

13:18.109 --> 13:19.739
And that's where we're going next.

13:19.739 --> 13:23.280
Validating the responses you get back from an LLM using this model.

13:23.280 --> 13:25.480
model_validate_json method from pydantic.

13:25.770 --> 13:27.432
And there you have it. Those

13:27.432 --> 13:29.370
are the basics of Pydantic data models.

13:29.890 --> 13:31.921
You saw how you can create a data model

13:31.921 --> 13:35.110
by defining a class that inherits from BaseModel,

13:35.671 --> 13:38.880
and then defining the fields and the data types for that class.

13:39.551 --> 13:41.571
And how you can populate an instance of your data model

13:41.571 --> 13:45.120
using either a Python dictionary or JSON data as input.

13:45.650 --> 13:48.240
In the next lesson, you're going to take these skills

13:48.240 --> 13:49.813
and validate the output you're getting

13:49.813 --> 13:51.650
back in the response from an LLM.

13:51.990 --> 13:53.571
I'll see you there.